fact WorkItem(id: "WI-984", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: THE SCOPEID TWIN — SCOPE-HOOD IS STILL UNTYPED HERE. Rustland's `intern.rs` has exactly the defect WI-976 removed from scaland: `ScopeInclusion.parent_scope_raw: u32` and `SymbolDef::Resolved`'s scope are bare integers, and every `SymbolTable` scope-keyed method takes one — so a scope, an arbitrary term and an arbitrary index are one type, and 'this raw is a scope' is a promise each caller carries. WORK: mirror WI-976 — an opaque `ScopeId` minted from a `Symbol`, threaded through the `SymbolTable` API and `SymbolDef::Resolved`. Note the shape scaland settled on and WHY, because the obvious alternative is worse: `ScopeId` wraps the SYMBOL, not the TermId. Wrapping the TermId keeps the functor projection a QUERY (fetch the term, match a nullary `Fn`, answer `Option`) — the runtime check the ticket exists to delete — while the symbol form makes it a total projection. Rustland is already halfway there: `KnowledgeBase` stores a rule's `domain: Symbol`. RIDER, and it is the cheap half: `instantiation_term_raw` is written by ~18 sites and read by NONE (`kb/load.rs` says so at its own writers, calling it vestigial); scaland dropped its copy in WI-976, so the two records have already diverged and keeping it buys no parity. ACCEPTANCE: cargo-test green; a scope cannot be built from a raw integer or a term, said as a compile error rather than a comment — say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Related: WI-976, WI-962, WI-983."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T11:20:08Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-984", name: "rustland")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "FROM WI-983 (delivered): DO NOT RESTATE THIS ACCEPTANCE AS A CALLER COUNT. WI-983's original acceptance was \"`scopeTerm` is left with exactly ONE caller in the tree\" and it was unreachable, contradicting that same ticket's own DO-NOT-CONVERT sentence. The miscount came from reading SIX loader assert sites as six pure conversions: at `entity_of` and at the provides-clause the ONE computed scope term fills a fact ARGUMENT as well as the domain, so typing the domain deletes four of the six calls, not six — and `provides_clause`'s `sort_ref`, a third term-level reader, had gone unlisted entirely. A site can serve two roles at once, so \"how many callers remain\" is the wrong shape for a criterion; \"no caller is in role X, and the rest are all role Y\" is the right one, and it is checkable. WI-984's own acceptance is already the better shape — a scope cannot be built from a raw integer or a term, said as a COMPILE ERROR — so keep it that way and resist tightening it into a count. Rustland is less exposed here regardless: `KnowledgeBase` already stores `domain: Symbol`, so it has no domain slot to convert; the term-level readers are the sites to leave alone.", at: "2026-08-04T08:28:17Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "CODE REVIEW OF WI-976/WI-983 (2026-08-04) FOUND A TRAP THIS TICKET SETS FOR ITSELF. The RIDER above says `instantiation_term_raw` is \"written by ~18 sites and read by NONE\" and invites deleting it, as scaland did. The READ claim checks out. The WRITE claim is the trap: rustland's producer is `build_instantiation_term(kb, …)` (`anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs:2998`), which takes `&mut KnowledgeBase` and has SIDE EFFECTS the field's deadness says nothing about — `kb.symbols.intern(&sort_name)` (:3164), `kb.alloc(Term::Ref/Fn)` (:3155, :3179), and `find_scope_by_name(..).unwrap_or_else(|| kb.make_name_term(&n))` (:3159). Delete the field, find the call dead, delete the call — and symbols plus name terms a `requires` declaration used to intern into the KB stop existing, changing `by_qualified_name` / `try_resolve_symbol` / `resolve_in_scope` answers SILENTLY. The field is dead; the CALL is not. Do the rider as two steps with a measurement between them: drop the field and keep the call, confirm `by_qualified_name`'s contents are unchanged over the stdlib load, and only then decide whether the interning is load-bearing or itself vestigial.", at: "2026-08-04T09:04:44Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "A DIVERGENCE TO RECORD BEFORE STARTING, not to undo (found by the same review). WI-976 moved SCALAND's scope key from a TermId raw to a SYMBOL raw. Rustland's is still a TermId raw: `intern.rs:198` (`SymbolDef::Resolved.scope_raw: u32`), `:255` (`ScopeInclusion.parent_scope_raw`) and `:397` (`define(…, scope_raw)`) are all fed TERM ids — see `kb/load.rs:2226` (`parent_scope_raw: current_scope.raw()`) and `:2747` (`parent_scope_raw: sort_term.raw()`). The two numbers index DIFFERENT arrays (Symbol -> `defs`, TermId -> the term store), so `resolve_recursive` / `internal_visible_from` / `add_parent` are no longer transcribable in either direction: a fix ported literally between the two trees keys the wrong table and yields a scope that resolves nothing — silently, with no type error, because both are `u32`. Note rustland is already internally split on this (scope keys are term raws while `by_domain` keys are `Symbol`s), so the symbol form this ticket asks for is the more coherent end state; the point is that DURING the port neither implementation's line numbers can be trusted as a guide to the other's.", at: "2026-08-04T09:04:48Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "FROM WI-990 (delivered, scaland): DO NOT PORT SCALAND'S MINT SHAPE WITHOUT READING WI-1004. Scaland's `ScopeId` mint now lives on `SymbolTable` (`scopeOf`) rather than on the companion, because a `TermSymbol` indexes ONE table's `defs` and the loader threads two. That move is the RIGHT layer and worth mirroring, but it does NOT close the cross-table hazard: the check it carries is a range refusal, which catches only the direction where the foreign table is the LARGER one — and in the loader the foreign table (`fileSym`) is always the smaller. The close that is a type is a path-dependent `opaque type ScopeId` as a member of the table (verified in Scala 3), filed as WI-1004; Rust has no path-dependent types, so the equivalent there is a distinct newtype per table or a table-id tag on `Symbol`, and it is a DIFFERENT design question from this ticket. Keep WI-984 to what it says — an opaque `ScopeId` minted from a `Symbol`, threaded through `SymbolTable` and `SymbolDef::Resolved` — and do not inherit scaland's range check as if it settled provenance.", at: "2026-08-04T20:15:52Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "FROM WI-1004 (DELIVERED, scaland) — WHAT THE PATH-DEPENDENT CLOSE COST, AND WHY IT DOES NOT MAKE THIS TICKET A PARITY ITEM. Scaland now has `class SymbolTable: opaque type ScopeId = TermSymbol` as a MEMBER, so `a.ScopeId` and `b.ScopeId` are distinct types and a scope minted by one table is a compile error at another's `resolveInScope` or at the KB's clause `domain` (measured: `Found: a.ScopeId  Required: b.ScopeId`, Scala 3.6.3). Rust has no path-dependent types, so this exact shape does NOT port — do not hold WI-984 for it, and do not widen WI-984's scope to chase it. THREE FACTS WORTH CARRYING ACROSS ANYWAY. (1) The cost was NOT a tag on the symbol: it was one type PARAMETER each on the three records that hold a scope with no table in scope — `SymbolDef` (covariant, so `Unresolved` stays a member of every table's), `ScopeInclusion`, and `KnowledgeBase`'s `RuleEntry` — plus dependent method types (`fn(kb, scope: kb.ScopeId)`) at ~30 loader signatures. The KB PINS ONE TABLE (`type ScopeId = symbols.ScopeId`) rather than each clause carrying a table of its own; that was the question to settle first and it sized everything else. (2) The close is over the USE, not the MINT, and that gap is REAL and stays open in scaland: `scopeOf` still takes a bare symbol, so `kb.symbols.scopeOf(fileSymSymbol)` type-checks, is in range, and silently names whatever KB symbol shares that index. Closing THAT needs the SYMBOL tagged with its table, which in both trees reaches the term store and the discrimination tree (they key on the symbol and have no table to speak of). Scaland asserts the hole rather than implying it is closed (`ScopeIdentityTest`, 'the mint refuses a symbol its table never issued'); a rust newtype-per-table would inherit exactly the same split. (3) The range check is therefore LOAD-BEARING, not vestigial — it is the only thing between a foreign symbol and an index panic in a display path — so if WI-984 mirrors `scope_of`, mirror the bound check with it. Delivered 2026-08-05.", at: "2026-08-05T03:02:45Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTED, workspace green 4223/0 plus 4 doctests. /code-review NOT YET RUN — it is user-invocable only; /simplify run instead (4 agents), findings applied.\n\nWHAT LANDED: opaque `intern::ScopeId` wrapping a Symbol, minted only by `SymbolTable::scope_id`. `SymbolDef::Resolved.scope`, `ScopeInclusion.parent_scope` and every scope-keyed SymbolTable method take it; `KnowledgeBase::scope_id_of(name_term)` bridges where the loader holds the scope TERM, and `Loader::current_scope_id()` names the loader's own derivation. The acceptance is three `compile_fail` doctests on `ScopeId` plus one positive — they run under `cargo test --doc`, which a doc comment in tests/ would not.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT JUSTIFIED THE SYMBOL FORM, and it was not theoretical: 227 of 2602 resolved stdlib symbols answered `None` from `declaring_scope_symbol`, and `scope(?s, ?r)` failed on each. They are one population — an entity's FIELDS are declared in the CONSTRUCTOR's scope, and `alloc` stores a constructor's nullary Fn as `Term::Ref` (WI-511), which the Fn-only match could not read. Same defect at `builtin_scope`, `scope_qualified_name`, `scope_display_name`, `internal_field_hidden_from` (an `internal` field of a constructor-owned scope read as VISIBLE) and the op-scope check in `emit_sort_info`.\n\nMY FIRST CONTROL WAS WRONG and driving it said so. I predicted the EPONYMOUS sort (`sort P { entity P(...) }`) would be the failing case; measured, that symbol is NOT marked a constructor, so its members answered before the change too. The test file now says so explicitly and drives the real population (a variant's field), with a non-constructor scope beside it as the pass-either-way row.\n\nA DEFECT I INTRODUCED, found by a review agent and driven with a control: `provides Stack[T = Int64] language anthill ... end` ABORTED the loader. `load_provides_block` assigned the APPLIED spec term as `current_scope`, documented as a per-instantiation scope IDENTITY. That identity was VACUOUS — nothing is ever defined into an applied term's scope and no add_parent links one, so BOTH instantiations resolved against an empty scope with no parents while the bare `provides Stack` beside them used Stack's real scope. Fixed to the base sort; the two spellings now agree, matching the domain, which already collapsed both. The existing `parameterized_provides_block_test` missed it because its blocks carry only language/artifact clauses, so no name is ever resolved inside — rows added there that put a body in and resolve a Stack-scoped name through it. That file's module doc PINNED the retired rule and is corrected.\n\nA DEFECT AT THE SEAM: `anthill.reflect.scope` has TWO backings and only one was fixed. Its stdlib signature says 'Symbol -> enclosing scope symbol (None for top-level)'; the SLD builtin now implements that, but the eval host op called `KnowledgeBase::scope_of`, a scan for a SIBLING sort/namespace/operation. MEASURED on one fixture the two shared NO answer: eval gave none/none/Tank.fill/none where SLD gave Full/Tank/wi984s/Tank. Both now read the declaring scope. `scope_of` had no other caller and is DELETED — a function named 'the scope of' that answered a sibling is a trap. WI-1016's test pinned only the some/none shape (its comment explained why); it now pins the symbol.\n\nTHE RIDER, done as the two steps the feedback prescribed. `instantiation_term_raw` is gone; the `build_instantiation_term` CALL stays and is documented at the function rather than the call site. MEASURED with the call deleted: `by_qualified_name`'s NAME SET is identical (2602 both ways, so no resolution answer moves) but 10 symbols stop existing — the short names of `requires`d specs — and 202 of 3336 survivors change RANK. Interning order is the fallback key for named-arg canonicalization on a schema-less functor, and the term ids advanced name the requirement slots (`__req_partialeq_14996` -> `__req_partialeq_14975`). So the interning is NOT vestigial; retiring the walk is its own measurable change. Side effect of dropping the field: two `requires` of one spec at different instantiations were two links and are now one — unobservable (resolve_in_scope and internal_visible_from read only parent_scope + is_enclosing), asserted rather than left silent.\n\nALSO FROM REVIEW: a THIRD `_unknown` scope-name fallback survived in `forbid_internal_import` — same query, converted; `_unknown` now has no producer. Two loader maps still keyed scopes on a raw u32 term (`type_param_vars`, `requires_seen`) — the ticket's own defect surviving inside the loader, retyped. `KnowledgeBase::global_scope()` replaces 19 hand-spelled incantations in two forms under four names, and `builtin_scope`'s `local_name(owner) == \"_global\"` short-name comparison became a ScopeId compare. ~110 loop-invariant derivations hoisted. `existing_namespace_in_scope` shares a lookup both scan arms spelled inline.\n\nWHAT THE ScopeId DOES NOT CLOSE, stated at the type rather than implied: (1) WHICH TABLE issued the symbol — the range check catches only the larger-foreign-table direction, asserted by `scope_id_refuses_a_symbol_this_table_never_issued`; closing it needs the symbol tagged, which WI-1004's feedback says is a different ticket. (2) `Symbol::from_raw` is pub const, so `scope_id(Symbol::from_raw(3))` compiles and, in range, succeeds — the compile errors say a scope cannot be built from an integer IN A SCOPE POSITION, not that an integer can never reach one, and that hop is one call long. I had over-claimed this before review.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT DONE: threading ScopeId through the ScopePass/ScopeSite spine so `scope_id_of` could be deleted (filed as WI-1028 — the loader holds 89 derivations and the terms it unwraps were built from symbols it already had); the term-level readers `is_sort_scope`/`eponymous_sort_symbol` (WI-983's feedback rules them out of scope — but WI-926's comment gave TWO reasons for not marking an eponymous constructor and this change made one of them FALSE, so the comment is corrected to rest on the one that holds).", at: "2026-08-06T11:19:31Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION, driven after delivery — a premise I wrote into the delivery record was FALSE, and the user's question 'why do we need persistent term ids?' is what refuted it.\n\nI kept the `build_instantiation_term` call and argued the interning was load-bearing because deleting it re-ranks 202 of 3336 symbols and renames requirement slots (`__req_partialeq_14996` -> `__req_partialeq_14975`). I MEASURED THAT THE IDS CHANGE and treated it as evidence that something DEPENDS on them. It is not the same claim. Checked: `persistence/term_ser.rs` writes NO numeric id — every reference is name-keyed — so no id crosses a process boundary; and the interning-order fallback in `canonicalize_record_named_args` needs all producers to agree WITHIN one KB, which they do whatever the order is, not to be stable ACROSS loads. The decisive experiment: delete the call, run the full workspace — 4223/0, including the golden CLI transcript and every persistence round trip. The walk was then DEAD (141 lines, no caller) and is deleted, with the same evidence applied to the namespace-term `alloc` in `ensure_intermediate_namespaces`.\n\nWI-1028 WAS MISFRAMED, and by exactly the defect this ticket's own feedback quotes. Its acceptance was '`scope_id_of` is deleted' — a caller count, which WI-983's feedback calls the wrong shape ('no caller is in role X, and the rest are all role Y' is the right one). I filed it one commit after delivering the ticket carrying that warning. Re-scoped, and the role split done inline instead: every PURE-SCOPE-ROLE site now takes a `ScopeId` (`scan_rule`, `scan_rule_goal`, `scan_operation_params`, `process_imports`, `ensure_intermediate_namespaces` including its return type, and `register_stdlib_scopes`, which minted 21 nullary terms whose only consumer was the projection back to their own functor). 89 derivations -> 29, and each of the 29 hands the same value on AS A TERM (`is_sort_scope`, `walk_scopes`, `register_prelude_constructor`, `enter_sort_with_body`, `assert_fact`). The criterion is written at `scope_id_of` itself so the next reader is not tempted back into counting callers. Workspace green after each step.", at: "2026-08-06T11:52:37Z")
